# The Tale of the First Harvest

In the time before memory, two siblings discovered the world's first grain seeds. The Elder Sister found them growing wild on the mountainside, while the Younger Brother learned the secret of cultivation from watching the birds.

The Elder Sister came to her brother with a proposal: "I will give you half my seeds if you promise to share your knowledge of planting. Together, we can feed our people through the coming winter."

The Brother hesitated. His knowledge was precious, earned through seasons of patient observation. What if she took the seeds and his wisdom, then kept the abundant harvest for herself?

But the Sister saw his doubt and said, "I offer my seeds first, before you teach me anything. This is my trust in you."

Moved by her gesture, the Brother shared everything he knew. When harvest came, it was more bountiful than either imagined. The Sister returned not half, but two-thirds of the yield to her brother, saying, "Your knowledge multiplied my seeds threefold. You deserve the greater share."

From that day forward, their people learned: trust given freely multiplies like grain in good soil, but trust withheld leaves everyone hungry.
